--TEST--
A `void` call as the guarded expression or an arm body of a `catch` expression is `E1020`, whether the expression is a statement, a value or inside a closure, and it reports once (`rule:expressions/catch-result-type`)
--FILE--
<?nvs
final class Job {
    public static function run(): void {
        throw new RuntimeError('no disk');
    }
    public static function count(): int {
        return 1;
    }
}

Job::run() catch (RuntimeError $e) => null;
var $kept = Job::run() catch (RuntimeError) => 1;
echo Job::run() catch (RuntimeError) => 1;
int $n = Job::count() catch (RuntimeError) => Job::run();
var $later = fn(): void => {
    Job::run() catch (RuntimeError) => null;
};
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E1020]: this `catch` expression uses a call that returns `void`
%A
11 | Job::run() catch (RuntimeError $e) => null;
%A
error[E1020]: this `catch` expression uses a call that returns `void`
%A
12 | var $kept = Job::run() catch (RuntimeError) => 1;
%A
error[E1020]: this `catch` expression uses a call that returns `void`
%A
13 | echo Job::run() catch (RuntimeError) => 1;
%A
error[E1020]: this `catch` expression uses a call that returns `void`
%A
14 | int $n = Job::count() catch (RuntimeError) => Job::run();
%A
error[E1020]: this `catch` expression uses a call that returns `void`
%A
16 |     Job::run() catch (RuntimeError) => null;
%A
error: aborting due to 5 errors
To read what a code means and how to fix it, run `nvs agent show <code>`.
